{"id":"86d7efc9-0b96-4997-889f-2f2b699a5966","object":"chat.completion","created":1789996716,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"schema\":\"amr-supervisor/v1\",\"select\":[{\"when\":\"OwnerFree and RequestA\",\"action\":\"SelectA\"},{\"when\":\"OwnerFree and RequestB\",\"action\":\"SelectB\"},{\"when\":\"True\",\"action\":\"Defer\"}],\"step\":[{\"when\":\"(not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked) and Halted\",\"action\":\"Brake\",\"next\":\"Stopped\"},{\"when\":\"not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked\",\"action\":\"Brake\",\"next\":\"BrakeRequested\"},{\"when\":\"ObservationUsable and TaskActive and not PedestrianBlocked and AtGoal and Released and Halted\",\"action\":\"Finish\",\"next\":\"Done\"},{\"when\":\"ObservationUsable and TaskActive and not PedestrianBlocked and AtGoal and Released\",\"action\":\"Brake\",\"next\":\"BrakeRequested\"},{\"when\":\"ObservationUsable and TaskActive and not PedestrianBlocked and not (AtGoal and Released) and OwnReservation and BodyClearOfZ\",\"action\":\"Release\",\"next\":\"Traversing\"},{\"when\":\"ModeBrakeRequested and not Halted\",\"action\":\"Brake\",\"next\":\"BrakeRequested\"},{\"when\":\"not OwnReservation and not Released\",\"action\":\"Request\",\"next\":\"Waiting\"},{\"when\":\"ModeStopped\",\"action\":\"Resume\",\"next\":\"ResumePending\"},{\"when\":\"True\",\"action\":\"Proceed\",\"next\":\"Traversing\"}]}","reasoning_content":"We need answer only JSON. Need synthesize select and step rules. Need obey exact keys schema select step. Need arrays ordered rules max 12 each. Need expressions using allowed booleans, not/and/or/parentheses, True False, names. Need select inputs: OwnerFree, RequestA, RequestB, PreferA, PreferB. Actions SelectA SelectB Defer. Requirements:\n- Selecting A requires OwnerFree and RequestA; selecting B requires OwnerFree and RequestB.\n- If reservation free and at least one request exists, select an eligible robot, rather than Defer.\n- Otherwise Defer required.\n- When both eligible, either legal; preferences no obligation, may conflict/absent.\n\nNeed provide total select. At most 12 rules. Need decide ordering. We can simply:\n1 when OwnerFree and RequestA -> SelectA? But if both requests and preferB? Policy says either legal, preferences don't impose additional acceptance obligation. Does preference maybe can be used? It says Prefer advisory. \"When both robots eligible, either is legal; preferences do not impose an additional acceptance obligation\" Could choose A always if RequestA even if B also and PreferB. Is that satisfying? Need evaluate selection eligibility and totality. It requires if OwnerFree and at least one request, select eligible robot rather than Defer. Selecting A requires OwnerFree and RequestA. If both A/B eligible, SelectA legal. If only B, need SelectB. So rules:\n1 when OwnerFree and RequestA -> SelectA\n2 when OwnerFree and RequestB -> SelectB\n3 True -> Defer\nThis works. But maybe if OwnerFree and RequestA and RequestB, rule 1 selects A. legal. Preferences irrelevant. Is there any hidden obligation to respect Prefer when both? It says preferences do not impose additional acceptance obligation. likely okay. Could include Preference to select preferred first? But if prefer conflicts? Simpler okay. Need exact expression strings. Could use \"OwnerFree and RequestA\". First rule. second \"OwnerFree and RequestB\". third \"True\". All valid. Need ensure no duplicate keys. At most 12.\n\nNow step is complex. Need synthesize ordered rules for policy P1. Need map first applicable requirement. There are 8 inputs: ObservationUsable, PedestrianBlocked, OwnReservation, Released, BodyClearOfZ, Halted, AtGoal, TaskActive. Mode flags: ModeIdle, ModeWaiting, ModeTraversing, ModeBrakeRequested, ModeStopped, ModeResumePending, ModeDone exactly one true. Step expressions may refer to these. Need actions and next mode as per priorities.\n\nNeed create rules covering all combinations. Need at most 12 rules. Need ensure first applicable. We can synthesize direct rules for each priority condition. Need handle within priority conditions different action/next depending on Halted etc. Need possibly combine.\n\nLet's formalize policy P1:\n\nInputs: O = ObservationUsable\nP = PedestrianBlocked\nR = OwnReservation (own res)\nRel = Released\nC = BodyClearOfZ\nH = Halted\nG = AtGoal\nT = TaskActive\nModes: MI, MW, MT, MB, MS, MRP?, MD. Let's name as given ModeIdle etc.\n\nPriority:\n1. An unusable observation, an inactive task, or a blocked pedestrian crossing requires Brake. If Halted true, next Stopped; else BrakeRequested. Condition B1 = (not ObservationUsable) or (not TaskActive) or PedestrianBlocked. If B1 then action Brake, next = H ? Stopped : BrakeRequested. This is first, regardless mode. Note if Halted true, action Brake not Finish even if AtGoal etc.\n2. With those blocking conditions absent, a robot that is AtGoal and has Released must establish stop confirmation before reporting completion: if not Halted, return Brake with next BrakeRequested; if Halted, return Finish with next Done. Condition B2 = not B1 and AtGoal and Released. Then if H -> Finish/Done else Brake/BrakeRequested.\n3. If neither previous applies and OwnReservation and BodyClearOfZ true, return Release with next Traversing. Condition B3 = not B1 and not (G and Rel) and R and C. action Release next Traversing. Priority even if current mode BrakeRequested.\n4. Otherwise, a robot in BrakeRequested with Halted false must keep Brake and next BrakeRequested. Condition B4 = not B1, not (G and Rel), not (R and C), ModeBrakeRequested, not H. action Brake next BrakeRequested.\n5. Otherwise, if both OwnReservation and Released are false, return Request with next Waiting. Condition B5 = after above, (not R and not Rel). action Request next Waiting. Note \"Otherwise\" after B4. It doesn't mention Halted? If both R and Rel false, return Request. If Halted? According to priority, yes unless B1 applicable (if no B1). But could be Halted true? It would Request Waiting. Hmm maybe okay. But B2 requires Released; if Rel false. B4 only BrakeRequested. So yes.\n6. Otherwise, if current mode is Stopped, return Resume with next ResumePending. Condition B6 = after above, ModeStopped. action Resume next ResumePending.\n7. In every remaining case return Proceed with next Traversing.\n\nNeed create ordered rules. We can implement priority conditions exactly. Need ensure all combinations result. Need no special terminal shortcut: applies in Done too. So rules don't exclude Done unless conditions. If mode Done and no B1/B2/B3/B4/B5/B6? Then Proceed next Traversing per P1. Is that intended? \"No special terminal-mode shortcut exists requirements apply in every mode including Done.\" Yes. We follow.\n\nNeed be careful about B2: \"With those blocking conditions absent, a robot that is AtGoal and has Released must establish stop confirmation before reporting completion: if not Halted, return Brake with next mode BrakeRequested; if Halted, return Finish with next mode Done.\" So condition B2 = not B1 and AtGoal and Released. If H true, action Finish next Done; if H false, Brake next BrakeRequested. Need split due H.\n\nB1 also split H? For B1: If H true, next Stopped; else BrakeRequested. Action Brake always. We can combine H? Need separate rules because next depends H. Could write two rules:\n1 when (not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked) and Halted -> Brake Stopped\n2 when (not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked) -> Brake BrakeRequested\nBut if H false. Need if H true first. Since H boolean exactly true/false. If we put H true rule before general, then general covers not H. Or general with not H. We can do:\n1 when (not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked) and Halted -> Brake next Stopped\n2 when not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked -> Brake next BrakeRequested\nBecause if H true, first catches. If H false, second. Valid.\n\nB2: Need not B1 and AtGoal and Released.\n3 when not B1 and AtGoal and Released and Halted -> Finish Done.\n4 when not B1 and AtGoal and Released -> Brake BrakeRequested (if H false due first)\nBut if H true, first B2 halt catches. If H false, fourth. Need include not H? Not necessary if order. But if H true and rule 3 catches. If H false, rule 3 false. Good. However if H true and B1 true, rule 1 catches before B2, so okay. Rule 3 can include not B1 to be explicit. Need define B1 = not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked. We can write expression.\n\nB3: not B1 and not (AtGoal and Released) and OwnReservation and BodyClearOfZ -> Release Traversing. Need ensure B2 absent. Since B2 condition is not B1 and AtGoal and Released. So not(B2) = B1 or not AtGoal or not Released. Under not B1, it's not AtGoal or not Released. We can include \"and not (AtGoal and Released)\" after not B1. Fine. Rule 5.\n\nB4: not B1, not B2, not B3, ModeBrakeRequested, not Halted -> Brake BrakeRequested. Since B4 \"Otherwise\" includes all previous not. Need express. But we can rely on ordering: after rules 1-5, if none matched, then B4 condition only needs ModeBrakeRequested and not Halted? Wait if B1 false, B2 false, B3 false. Yes because earlier rules would have matched if conditions true. In first-match ordered rules, for rule 6 we can just condition \"ModeBrakeRequested and not Halted\" because any case satisfying previous would have been caught. But be careful B2 if H true? If B2 and H true would be caught by rule 3. If B2 and H false caught by rule 4. So after those, B2 false. B1 false. B3 false. Thus yes. Similarly B5 can be just \"not OwnReservation and not Released\" after B4? Need B4 condition includes ModeBrakeRequested and not H. If not R and not Rel and ModeBrakeRequested and not H, B4 would have priority and should Brake, not Request. Our B4 rule if before B5 will catch that. So B5 can be just not R and not Rel. Since B4 already caught relevant. Good.\n\nB6: after B5, if ModeStopped -> Resume. Can just condition ModeStopped.\n\nB7: True -> Proceed Traversing.\n\nNeed ensure B4 condition \"ModeBrakeRequested and not Halted\" maybe if B1 true and H false, B1 caught. If B2 true and H false, B2 caught. If B3 true, caught. Good.\n\nNeed count rules. Let's propose:\n1: (not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked) and Halted -> Brake, Stopped\n2: not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked -> Brake, BrakeRequested\n3: not (not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked) and AtGoal and Released and Halted -> Finish, Done\n4: not (not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked) and AtGoal and Released -> Brake, BrakeRequested\n5: not (not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked) and not (AtGoal and Released) and OwnReservation and BodyClearOfZ -> Release, Traversing\n6: ModeBrakeRequested and not Halted -> Brake, BrakeRequested\n7: not OwnReservation and not Released -> Request, Waiting\n8: ModeStopped -> Resume, ResumePending\n9: True -> Proceed, Traversing\n\n9 rules <=12. Need check if rule 6 before B5 could swallow B3? B3 condition might include ModeBrakeRequested and H false and OwnReservation BodyClear etc. But B3 is rule 5 before rule 6, so caught. Good. B2 caught before. B1 caught.\n\nBut wait rule 6 condition \"ModeBrakeRequested and not Halted\" after B3. What if B5 condition (not R and not Rel) and ModeBrakeRequested and not H: Policy priority 4 says \"Otherwise, a robot in BrakeRequested with Halted false must keep Brake...\" This comes before 5. So yes rule 6 catches and returns Brake, not Request. Good.\n\nWhat if B6 ModeStopped and not R and not Rel? B5 before B6 catches Request Waiting. Policy priority 5 before 6. Yes.\n\nWhat if ModeStopped and ModeBrakeRequested? one-hot exactly one, cannot both. But expressions don't infer? They say exactly one such mode flag true. The model interpreter likely enforces? \"Exactly one such mode flag is true.\" We can rely? Maybe yes. But \"Do not infer relationships between Boolean inputs beyond the one-hot mode flags.\" We can rely one-hot mode flags. But no need.\n\nPotential issues: We didn't include ModeIdle etc in rules except ModeBrakeRequested, ModeStopped. P1 references current mode only in B4 (BrakeRequested) and B6 (Stopped). Good.\n\nNeed check B5 \"Otherwise, if both OwnReservation and Released are false, return Request with next mode Waiting.\" Does it apply if Halted true? Suppose no B1, B2 false (G/Rel false), B3 false, B4 false (mode not MB or H true), B5 true (R false Rel false). It returns Request Waiting even if H true. Our rule 7 does. Good.\n\nNeed check B3 condition \"If neither previous applies and OwnReservation and BodyClearOfZ true, return Release with next mode Traversing.\" It says neither previous applies = not B1 and not B2. We included. It doesn't require not B5 etc. Good. It takes priority even if current mode BrakeRequested. Our rule 5 before 6. Good. If H true? B3 no H condition. So if B3 true and H true, return Release Traversing. Our rule 5 catches H true too (unless B1/B2). Is that correct? Priority 3 after B1/B2. Yes. But wait if H true, and OwnReservation BodyClear, no B1/B2, should Release. Halted is trusted stop confirmation. Could be stopped but release. Yes.\n\nNeed check B1 expression: \"An unusable observation, an inactive task, or a blocked pedestrian crossing requires Brake.\" That is (not ObservationUsable) or (not TaskActive) or PedestrianBlocked. If Halted true, next Stopped, else BrakeRequested. Our rules split. If H true and also B2 AtGoal Released, B1 priority 1 so Brake Stopped, not Finish. Rule 1 catches. Good.\n\nPotential problem: Rule 2 \"not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked\" without \"and not Halted\". If H true, rule 1 catches. If H false, rule 2 catches. Since H boolean, fine. But if H true and rule 1 expression true, first. Good. If H true but rule 1 false? impossible if B1 true. Good.\n\nRule 3: \"not (not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked) and AtGoal and Released and Halted -> Finish Done\". Need ensure B1 false. Equivalent ObservationUsable and TaskActive and not PedestrianBlocked. Could write \"ObservationUsable and TaskActive and not PedestrianBlocked and AtGoal and Released and Halted\". Shorter. Rule 4 same without Halted. This may be clearer. Need no not of disjunction? Both valid. Use shorter maybe. Need watch expression length. Fine.\n\nLet's design expressions exactly.\n\nSelect:\n[\n {\"when\":\"OwnerFree and RequestA\",\"action\":\"SelectA\"},\n {\"when\":\"OwnerFree and RequestB\",\"action\":\"SelectB\"},\n {\"when\":\"True\",\"action\":\"Defer\"}\n]\n\nStep:\n1 {\"when\":\"(not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked) and Halted\",\"action\":\"Brake\",\"next\":\"Stopped\"}\n2 {\"when\":\"not ObservationUsable or not TaskActive or PedestrianBlocked\",\"action\":\"Brake\",\"next\":\"BrakeRequested\"}\n3 {\"when\":\"ObservationUsable and TaskActive and not PedestrianBlocked and AtGoal and Released and Halted\",\"action\":\"Finish\",\"next\":\"Done\"}\n4 {\"when\":\"ObservationUsable and TaskActive and not PedestrianBlocked and AtGoal and Released\",\"action\":\"Brake\",\"next\":\"BrakeRequested\"}\n5 {\"when\":\"ObservationUsable and TaskActive and not PedestrianBlocked and not (AtGoal and Released) and OwnReservation and BodyClearOfZ\",\"action\":\"Release\",\"next\":\"Traversing\"}\n6 {\"when\":\"ModeBrakeRequested and not Halted\",\"action\":\"Brake\",\"next\":\"BrakeRequested\"}\n7 {\"when\":\"not OwnReservation and not Released\",\"action\":\"Request\",\"next\":\"Waiting\"}\n8 {\"when\":\"ModeStopped\",\"action\":\"Resume\",\"next\":\"ResumePending\"}\n9 {\"when\":\"True\",\"action\":\"Proceed\",\"next\":\"Traversing\"}\n\nNeed verify priority with rules 3/4 and B1 false. Rule 3 uses positive B1 false. Rule 5 uses positive B1 false and not(AtGoal and Released). But what if B1 true? Rule 1/2 catch. Good. Rule 6 after rule 5. Rule 6 condition doesn't explicitly exclude B3/B2/B1. But if any previous true, first match. So for cases reaching 6, those false. Good.\n\nNeed verify B2 if H false: rule 4 condition positive B1 false and AtGoal Released. If H true, rule 3 catches. If H false, rule 4 catches. Good. If H true and B1 false, rule 3 catches. If H false, rule 3 false due Halted. Good.\n\nPotential issue: Rule 4 condition lacks \"not Halted\" but if H true, rule 3 catches. If H true but B1 true, rule 1 catches. So no issue.\n\nPotential issue: Rule 6 \"ModeBrakeRequested and not Halted\" after rule 5. What if current mode BrakeRequested, H false, B1 false, B2 false, B3 false, but B5 true (not R not Rel). Policy priority 4 says keep Brake before 5. Our rule 6 catches. Good. What if B3 true? rule 5 catches. What if B2 true? rule 4 catches. Good.\n\nPotential issue: Rule 7 \"not OwnReservation and not Released\" after rule 6. What if mode Stopped and not R not Rel and H false? Rule 6 only if ModeBrakeRequested. So rule 7 catches Request Waiting. Policy priority 5 before 6. Yes. If mode Stopped and R true Rel true? Rule 7 false, rule 8 catches Resume. Policy priority 6. If mode Stopped and R true Rel false? B5? both false? no, R true so B5 false. B3? R and C maybe true? If C true, B3 catches. If C false, B4? mode not MB. B6 ModeStopped catches Resume. Policy: priority 3 if OwnReservation and BodyClear; if C false, neither prev, B4 no, B5 no (R true), B6 Stopped -> Resume. Yes. If mode Stopped and R false Rel true? B5 false. B3? R false. B6 catches Resume. Policy: B5 requires both R and Rel false; no. B6 yes. Resume. Good.\nIf mode Idle and R false Rel false: rule 7 Request Waiting. Policy B5. Yes.\nIf mode Idle and R true Rel true and C false and G false: B3 false, B4 false, B5 false, B6 false, rule 9 Proceed Traversing. Policy priority 7. Is that correct? OwnReservation true, Released true, BodyClearOfZ false, not AtGoal. No release because body not clear. Not request because own true. Not stopped. Proceed. Yes. Might seem odd if released true but body not clear, proceed? Policy says remaining Proceed. Good.\n\nNeed consider \"Released denotes an acknowledged reservation release for its task; it is distinct from BodyClearOfZ\". If OwnReservation true and Released true and BodyClearOfZ false, B3 false. AtGoal? If AtGoal true and Released true, B2 applies (if no B1) regardless BodyClearOfZ. B2 requires AtGoal and Released. It would Brake/Finish before B3. That seems per policy: B2 before B3. If AtGoal true and Released true and H false, Brake (BrakeRequested) even if OwnReservation BodyClearOfZ. Wait policy: \"With blocking conditions absent, a robot that is AtGoal and has Released must establish stop confirmation before reporting completion: if not Halted, return Brake... if Halted, Finish...\" This is priority 2. Then priority 3 Release if OwnReservation and BodyClearOfZ. So if AtGoal and Released and OwnReservation BodyClear, B2 applies before B3, so Brake/Finish, not Release. Our rules: rule 3/4 before rule 5. Yes.\n\nNeed check if B2 H true and OwnReservation BodyClear: Finish Done, not Release. Rule 3 catches before rule 5. Good.\n\nPotential issue: B1 \"unusable observation, inactive task, or blocked pedestrian crossing requires Brake. If Halted true, next Stopped; otherwise BrakeRequested.\" We set action Brake even if H true. It says requires Brake. If Halted true, next mode Stopped. Action still Brake. Good. Could there be \"Halted true\" means already stopped, but policy explicitly Brake with next Stopped. We follow.\n\nNeed ensure JSON exactly one object no markdown. Need root keys schema, select, step. No extra. Need all strings valid. Need action values exactly. next values exactly without Mode prefix: Idle, Waiting, Traversing, BrakeRequested, Stopped, ResumePending, Done. We use Stopped, BrakeRequested, Done, Traversing, Waiting, ResumePending. Good.\n\nNeed ensure no duplicate JSON keys. Each rule object has when/action/next. select rules have when/action. No duplicate within object.\n\nNeed ensure at most 12 rules each. select 3, step 9. Total branches? \"At most 32 total branches are allowed; per-function limit twelve still applies.\" Branches maybe rules? total 12? Hmm \"At most 32 total branches are allowed\" likely total rules across both functions? 3+9=12 <=32. Fine.\n\nNeed ensure source size <=200KB. Fine.\n\nNeed consider expression syntax: allowed names only permitted Boolean names. We use ObservationUsable, TaskActive, PedestrianBlocked, AtGoal, Released, Halted, OwnReservation, BodyClearOfZ, ModeBrakeRequested, ModeStopped. Good. \"not (AtGoal and Released)\" uses parentheses. Python Boolean precedence: not > and > or. Our expressions with parentheses okay.\n\nNeed maybe include all mode flags? Step expressions may refer to ModeIdle, ModeWaiting, ModeTraversing, ModeBrakeRequested, ModeStopped, ModeResumePending, ModeDone. We only use two. Fine.\n\nNeed think about independent model interpreter and source/model correspondence. They evaluate all combos and compare policy. We need be certain no flaw in using positive B1 false expressions. Let's do exhaustive manual for all cases maybe.\n\nLet's formalize policy function step(mode, facts):\nif B1 = !O or !T or P:\n   if H: Brake->Stopped else Brake->BrakeRequested\nelif G and Rel:\n   if H: Finish->Done else Brake->BrakeRequested\nelif R and C:\n   Release->Traversing\nelif mode == BrakeRequested and !H:\n   Brake->BrakeRequested\nelif !R and !Rel:\n   Request->Waiting\nelif mode == Stopped:\n   Resume->ResumePending\nelse:\n   Proceed->Traversing\n\nOur rules:\n1 if B1 and H: Brake Stopped\n2 if B1: Brake BrakeRequested\n3 if !B1 and G and Rel and H: Finish Done\n4 if !B1 and G and Rel: Brake BrakeRequested\n5 if !B1 and !(G and Rel) and R and C: Release Traversing\n6 if mode==BrakeRequested and !H: Brake BrakeRequested\n7 if !R and !Rel: Request Waiting\n8 if mode==Stopped: Resume ResumePending\n9 True: Proceed Traversing\n\nCheck cases:\n- B1 true H false: rule1 false, rule2 true -> Brake BR. Policy: Brake BR. ok.\n- B1 true H true: rule1 true -> Brake Stopped. ok.\n- B1 false, B2 true H true: rule1 false, rule2 false, rule3 true -> Finish Done. ok.\n- B1 false, B2 true H false: rule3 false, rule4 true -> Brake BR. ok.\n- B1 false, B2 false, B3 true: rule5 true (since !B1 and !(G&Rel) and R&C). ok. Note if mode MB and H false, rule5 before rule6. Policy B3 before B4. ok.\n- B1 false, B2 false, B3 false, B4 true (mode MB !H): rule6 true. ok.\n- B1 false, B2 false, B3 false, B4 false, B5 true (!R & !Rel): rule7 true unless rule6? B4 false if not (mode MB !H). If mode MB !H and B5 true, B4 should true because B4 condition only mode MB !H, regardless R/Rel. Wait B4 comes before B5. Our rule6 condition mode MB !H catches even if B5 true. So rule7 not reached. Policy: if B4 true, Brake. Correct. If B4 false, rule7 true. ok.\n- B1 false, B2 false, B3 false, B4 false, B5 false, B6 true (mode Stopped): rule8 true unless earlier. ok.\n- remaining: rule9. ok.\n\nWait B4 in policy: \"Otherwise, a robot in BrakeRequested with Halted false must keep Brake and next mode BrakeRequested.\" It is after B3. It does not require B5 false. So if B5 true, B4 still applies. Our rule6 before rule7. Good.\n\nPotential subtlety: In policy step, requirement 5 says \"Otherwise, if both OwnReservation and Released are false, return Request with next mode Waiting.\" This is after requirement 4. So if mode BrakeRequested and H false, even if both R/Rel false, B4 applies. Our rule6 catches. Good.\n\nPotential subtlety: Requirement 6 \"Otherwise, if the current mode is Stopped, return Resume with next mode ResumePending.\" If mode Stopped and both R/Rel false, B5 applies before B6. Our rule7 before rule8. Good.\n\nPotential subtlety: Requirement 7 \"In every remaining case return Proceed with next mode Traversing.\" If current mode Done and B5 false etc, Proceed. Our rule9. Good.\n\nLooks correct.\n\nBut let's consider select. Policy: \"If a reservation is free and at least one request exists, select an eligible robot, rather than Defer. Otherwise Defer is required. When both robots are eligible, either is legal; preferences do not impose an additional acceptance obligation, and their values may conflict or both be absent.\"\n\nOur select:\n1 OwnerFree & RequestA -> SelectA\n2 OwnerFree & RequestB -> SelectB\n3 True -> Defer\n\nCheck all combos:\n- OwnerFree false: rule1 false, rule2 false, rule3 Defer. Required Defer because reservation not free even if requests. ok.\n- OwnerFree true, RequestA false, RequestB false: Defer. Required.\n- OwnerFree true, RequestA true, RequestB false: SelectA. A eligible. ok.\n- OwnerFree true, RequestA false, RequestB true: rule1 false, rule2 SelectB. ok.\n- OwnerFree true, both true: rule1 SelectA. Both eligible; either legal. Preferences irrelevant. Is there any requirement that if both eligible and PreferB true, must select B? \"PreferA/PreferB are advisory. ... preferences do not impose an additional acceptance obligation, and their values may conflict or both be absent.\" Advisory likely means can use but not must. \"When both robots are eligible, either is legal\" so SelectA legal. Good.\n\nCould there be a hidden test where both eligible and PreferB true, expects SelectB? The phrase \"preferences do not impose an additional acceptance obligation\" maybe means preferences don't force. So either legal. They likely accept any legal. But if they compare exact? The evaluation uses independent model interpreter and source/model correspondence checking. The policy might be nondeterministic? They may allow set of valid outputs. Need ensure our choice is within. Yes.\n\nNeed output exact JSON. No markdown. Use double quotes.\n\nLet's ensure no trailing commas.\n\nNeed maybe use \"schema\":\"amr-supervisor/v1\". Root keys exactly schema, select, step. Order maybe schema, select, step. JSON object.\n\nNeed no comments.\n\nLet's produce final JSON.\n\nDouble-check expression strings for rule 5: \"ObservationUsable and TaskActive and not PedestrianBlocked and not (AtGoal and Released) and OwnReservation and BodyClearOfZ\". Python precedence: not (AtGoal and Released) okay. It means !B1? B1 = !O or !T or P. Positive B1 false = O and T and !P. Yes. Good.\n\nRule 3: \"ObservationUsable and TaskActive and not PedestrianBlocked and AtGoal and Released and Halted\". If H true. Good.\n\nRule 4: same without Halted. If H false? If H true, rule3 catches. If H true but B1 true? rule1 catches. If H true and B1 false and G Rel false, rule4? It doesn't include not H, so if H true and B1 false and G Rel true, rule3 would catch. If H true and B1 false and G Rel false, rule4 false. So no issue. If H true and B1 false and G Rel true, rule3 catches. Good. If H true and B1 false and G Rel true but rule3? yes. So rule4 not reached. Good.\n\nRule 6: \"ModeBrakeRequested and not Halted\". It does not include not B1/B2/B3, but earlier rules handle. If B1 true H false, rule2 catches. If B1 true H true, rule1 catches. If B2 true H false, rule4 catches. If B2 true H true, rule3 catches. If B3 true, rule5 catches. Good.\n\nRule 7: \"not OwnReservation and not Released\". Earlier rules handle B1/B2/B3/B4. But what if B1 false, B2 false, B3 false, B4 false, B5 true, mode Stopped? rule7 catches before rule8. Policy B5 before B6. Good.\n\nRule 8: \"ModeStopped\". Earlier rules handle B5. If B5 false and mode Stopped, Resume. Good.\n\nRule 9: True. Good.\n\nPotential issue: In step, action \"Release\" next \"Traversing\" per policy. We use \"Release\". Good.\nAction \"Request\" next \"Waiting\". Good.\nAction \"Resume\" next \"ResumePending\". Good.\nAction \"Proceed\" next \"Traversing\". Good.\nAction \"Brake\" next \"Stopped\" or \"BrakeRequested\". Good.\nAction \"Finish\" next \"Done\". Good.\n\nNeed ensure mode flag names exactly: ModeBrakeRequested, ModeStopped. Yes. Inputs: OwnReservation, BodyClearOfZ. Yes.\n\nLet's final."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1128,"completion_tokens":7200,"total_tokens":8328,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":6894},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":0,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":1128},"system_fingerprint":"aeb56401ca74e127821c4f9126dcb669"}